Q: How can I convert a JSON string, containing data for multiple dates, for use with the &quot;popups&quot; option in Vanilla Calendar Pro? A: <p>You can transform the data received from your database in multiple
ways, for instance using <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/reduce" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Array#reduce</a>:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>   .....
   popups: response.reduce((popups, {date, desc}) =&gt;
       ({...popups, [date]: {modifier: 'stylePopup', html: `&lt;div&gt;${desc}&lt;/div&gt;`}}), {})
</code></pre>
<p>Example <a href="https://jsfiddle.net/Lnjb251w/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">jsFiddle</a> or
stack snippet (might experience network issues):</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="false" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const response = [{"date":"2026-05-20","desc":"Test #1"},{"date":"2026-05-25","desc":"Test #2"}];
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () =&gt; {
    const { Calendar } = window.VanillaCalendarPro;
    const calendar = new Calendar('#calendar', {
        type: 'multiple',
        displayMonthsCount: 3,
        monthsToSwitch: 1,
        firstWeekday: 0,
        disableDatesPast: true,
        selectionDatesMode: 'multiple-ranged',
        popups: response.reduce((popups, {date, desc}) =&gt;
            ({...popups, [date]: {modifier: 'stylePopup', html: `&lt;div&gt;${desc}&lt;/div&gt;`}}), {})
    });
    calendar.init();
});</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div id="calendar"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/vanilla-calendar-pro@3.1.0/styles/index.min.css"&gt;
&lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/vanilla-calendar-pro@3.1.0/index.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: A Python Number Guessing game A: <p>You don't want recursion here. Don't use <code>exit()</code>. A more appropriate structure would obviate the need for that.</p>
<p>Try to avoid using globals.</p>
<p>Break down the code into discrete functions. It may mean writing more lines of code but you'll benefit from maintainability.</p>
<p>Make things like input selections parameter driven. Doing so makes your code more extensible when, for example, you want to add more options.</p>
<p>Here's a complete re-write that exemplifies the ideas proposed:</p>
<pre><code>from random import randint
from typing import Callable


def get_int(prompt: str) -&gt; int:
    while True:
        try:
            return int(input(prompt))
        except ValueError:
            pass
        print(&quot;Not a valid integer&quot;)


def play(p: dict[str, int]) -&gt; None:
    _tries = p[&quot;tries&quot;]
    _limit = p[&quot;limit&quot;]

    print(f&quot;Guess a number between 1 and {_limit}&quot;)
    print(f&quot;You have {_tries} tries&quot;)

    number = randint(1, _limit)

    for _try in range(1, _tries + 1):
        guess = get_int(&quot;Enter your guess: &quot;)
        if guess == number:
            print(f&quot;You won with {_tries-_try} tries remaining&quot;)
            break
        print(&quot;Too low&quot; if guess &lt; number else &quot;Too high&quot;)
    else:
        print(f&quot;You lost. the number was {number}&quot;)


def option() -&gt; Callable | None:
    for i, (prompt, _) in enumerate(MAIN_OPTIONS, 1):
        print(f&quot;{i}. {prompt}&quot;)
    while True:
        opt = get_int(&quot;&gt;&gt; &quot;)
        if 1 &lt;= opt &lt;= len(MAIN_OPTIONS):
            return MAIN_OPTIONS[opt - 1][1]
        print(&quot;Invalid option&quot;)


def change(p: dict[str, int]) -&gt; None:
    for i, (prompt, _) in enumerate(CHANGE_OPTIONS, 1):
        print(f&quot;{i}. {prompt}&quot;)
    while True:
        opt = get_int(&quot;&gt;&gt; &quot;)
        if 1 &lt;= opt &lt;= len(CHANGE_OPTIONS):
            CHANGE_OPTIONS[opt - 1][1](p)
            break
        print(&quot;Invalid option&quot;)


def tries(p: dict[str, int]) -&gt; None:
    while True:
        t = get_int(&quot;New number of tries: &quot;)
        if t &gt; 0:
            p[&quot;tries&quot;] = t
            break
        print(&quot;Must be greater than zero&quot;)


def limit(p: dict[str, int]) -&gt; None:
    while True:
        t = get_int(&quot;New max number: &quot;)
        if t &gt; 1:
            p[&quot;limit&quot;] = t
            break
        print(&quot;Must be greater than one&quot;)


MAIN_OPTIONS: list[tuple[str, Callable | None]] = [
    (&quot;Change settings&quot;, change),
    (&quot;Play the game&quot;, play),
    (&quot;Exit&quot;, None),
]

CHANGE_OPTIONS: list[tuple[str, Callable]] = [
    (&quot;Change tries&quot;, tries),
    (&quot;Change max number&quot;, limit),
]

if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:
    params = {&quot;tries&quot;: 5, &quot;limit&quot;: 50}

    while (func := option()) is not None:
        func(params)
</code></pre>
